gemini on glm found the round's real spec gap:

  "In `tx.commit().map_err(...)`, attributing failure to `index: 0` highlights an underdetermined
   edge case ... If all row inserts succeed but COMMIT fails, the failure belongs to the
   transaction as a whole rather than a specific input element. The `Rejected` type requires an
   element index where none exists."

Mine. `Rejected { index, cause }` has no transaction-level variant, so every arm had to name an
innocent row. Filed.

It also names why glm's shape is the durable one: glm delegates each row to the existing
`insert_run` inside the transaction, so "if the schema or run state mapping evolves, the batch
writer cannot silently drift from single-row writes" -- a property no test asserts and no count
shows.

DECISION. glm on 12 tests to 9, on that delegation, and on gemini being out of scope. Its five
clippy warnings were four `&[x.clone()]` slice lints in test code plus the summary; I fixed them
on merge and said so rather than merging a regression against a zero baseline.

